thacademy/question-capture/13981717/assets/a591a3637ae27dc219f4dd6d057b6f0722a678c68a522e2bfa8eda36af53f1c3.png) respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981717/assets/bddc2c0630b67cd0b8af4ef80d2c35c8680fe72626f05599a7e5901d9a451dbd.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981717/assets/630f8f1ca117b18c64d111d9267e19b5f3ac3626bfeaacdb77c20817acf6590d.png) which can also be written as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981717/assets/637b81b019362d0faa00288750ec2225cb6a13c3b2ad4b3e8cdf5f47dd63de64.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae29754a-f4ae-55bc-9815-198322472e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282332} {:answer/id #uuid "608ec1a3-f0bd-5dde-8611-bc06173014ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282333} {:answer/id #uuid "c855dd8c-530b-5d17-87aa-18fe880e787a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<-\\frac{13}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282334} {:answer/id #uuid "adbfa317-4555-51c4-a599-43cb89dc4fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-\\frac{13}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282335} {:answer/id #uuid "42e9e991-5e78-5c98-b028-e6203732edbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{7}<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c855dd8c-530b-5d17-87aa-18fe880e787a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<-\\frac{13}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282334} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d196156-79fa-5803-a1ac-b73953a9852e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5867fe9-8220-5380-a626-5393f33416ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-102968" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{7}⁡(x+2)+1<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x+2)+1 & <0 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x+2) & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $7.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+2)} & <{7}^{-1} \\\\ x+2 & <\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ x & <-\\frac{13}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+2>0\\,⇒\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<-\\frac{13}{7},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{13}{7}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{13}{7})\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(-2,-\\frac{13}{7})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-2,-\\frac{13}{7}),$ which can also be written as $-2<x<-\\frac{13}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8f2d68-d93f-5798-8f58-d89d9338fac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\le x<\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282339} {:answer/id #uuid "2277e36e-12ff-5d9b-a15c-bb29ccf26b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282340} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4c6e18-a0c0-556e-8020-4736d98443e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282341} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffdf6c2-b95b-5a30-a5ef-aa909c380f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9<x\\le \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282342} {:answer/id #uuid "609117d3-b93e-58af-a207-81c5202a5c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c8f2d68-d93f-5798-8f58-d89d9338fac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\le x<\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282339} :answer-field/id #uuid "d998cfb9-0a42-5a47-a253-d11873c550e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebaa80d4-6857-5232-aa65-fdb81f2c5033" :question/math-academy-id "q-102983" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(10-3x)\\le \\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(10-3x)} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9)} \\\\ 10-3x & \\le x+9 \\\\ -4x & \\le -1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ x+9 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{1}{4},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{1}{4},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x<\\frac{10}{3}$ and $x>-9,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{10}{3})$ and $(-9,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,\\frac{10}{3})\\cap (-9,\\infty )=[\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{10}{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{10}{3}),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{1}{4}\\le x<\\frac{10}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2861d7c4-b9b9-54f2-aa6c-6fe780de12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2861d7c4-b9b9-54f2-aa6c-6fe780de12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282346} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb70f8b2-8014-52a9-8c5d-525374a39b54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183ae3bb-1982-57bd-8628-538acf2ff08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-e^9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "183ae3bb-1982-57bd-8628-538acf2ff08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-e^9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282348} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e2e353-b218-5d39-9ad1-639bf741bff4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "936e960d-35aa-5c3e-b8af-3eb2dba0898a" :question/math-academy-id "q-278589" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7-x)-3\\ge 6.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7-x)-3 & \\ge 6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(7-x) & \\ge 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7-x)} & \\ge {e}^{9} \\\\ 7-x & \\ge {e}^{9} \\\\ -x & \\ge {e}^{9}-7 \\\\ x & \\le 7-{e}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n7-x>0\\,⇒\\,x<7\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 7-{e}^{9},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,7-{e}^{9}].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x<7,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,7).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,7-{e}^{9}]\\cap (-\\infty ,7)=(-\\infty ,7-{e}^{9}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,7-{e}^{9}],$ which can also be written as $x\\le 7-{e}^{9}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4dadd63-cc2e-5654-9032-a65b546a52a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3e^{5} - 1} \\le x \\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156323} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4521ed-3bfe-5a26-b230-4ef92cee4eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge \\frac{2}{3e^{5} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156324} {:answer/id #uuid "1c77ddb8-4c65-5ffc-916b-d1d4f4929f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3e^{5} - 1} \\le x \\le \\frac{2}{3e^{5} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156325} {:answer/id #uuid "50a7b132-dd12-55dc-ac4e-a5f6c5e71dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le \\frac{2}{3e^{5} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156326} {:answer/id #uuid "52d64a76-76d1-5266-8076-d36869ce6af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0 < x \\le \\frac{2}{3e^{5} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d64a76-76d1-5266-8076-d36869ce6af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0 < x \\le \\frac{2}{3e^{5} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156327} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9b49ec-fd77-5353-8996-5baf98f9f4e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c25ae1f2-3afc-5e1a-9d69-5f676e0bbd4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87745" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-5\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-5} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}\\cdot {e}^{-5} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} \\\\ (x+2){e}^{-5} & \\ge 3x \\\\ x+2 & \\ge 3{e}^{5}x \\\\ 2 & \\ge (3{e}^{5}-1)x \\\\ \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1} & \\ge x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-2 \\\\ 3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-2$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}]\\cap (-2,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=(0,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (0,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}],$ which can also be written as $0<x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0903ae82-9c06-5abb-9138-f2f60a22f909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1+3e}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282351} {:answer/id #uuid "520e7762-3dca-5e5b-af4a-4a67e4b8667f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282352} {:answer/id #uuid "f582caf4-14a0-52f6-9bf8-2e3c4e938372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<x\\le \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282353} {:answer/id #uuid "1602786b-c538-5b61-baef-6f5f8df4dbf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282354} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0b7e6e-9529-513d-9737-6d07b38a940d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1+{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1602786b-c538-5b61-baef-6f5f8df4dbf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282354} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6cc622d-2b0e-5636-a578-c8e88bf0d407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8054d64c-91b2-5437-a256-61188f1e6a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-88004" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+12\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+12 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & \\ge {e}^{-3} \\\\ x+1 & \\ge {e}^{-3} \\\\ x & \\ge {e}^{-3}-1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+1>0\\,⇒\\,x>-1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=[\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e80b13b5-5840-56e3-b6f5-cc481887ec50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282358} {:answer/id #uuid "fe65bb9d-1190-5f2c-8c8a-42afe7c89e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282359} {:answer/id #uuid "6586efb9-e75d-519c-ba3a-1b4eb15f1ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282360} {:answer/id #uuid "33db7b63-5722-59f5-ab36-c04e1473b391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282361} {:answer/id #uuid "13aae195-f8eb-52e3-9e18-2b8d71143ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13aae195-f8eb-52e3-9e18-2b8d71143ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282362} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fedd3e7-81fd-5936-bb09-968b3690b67b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd3d3031-06a8-5828-9587-d6c7cd3936bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-88005" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+28<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+28 & <0 \\\\ 7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & <-28 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)} & <{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2x & <{e}^{-4} \\\\ x & <\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2} \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n2x>0\\,⇒\\,x>0\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(0,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}})\\cap (0,\\infty )=(0,\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}),$ which can also be written as $0<x<\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c98c5a-c74d-5d93-9c29-610e44a3268b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282365} {:answer/id #uuid "43f6dda3-7144-54df-9e6f-2d8ce7106d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282366} {:answer/id #uuid "3a325fb6-bbf1-503f-9b99-70745ddc45fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282367} {:answer/id #uuid "854b2cab-3ca7-5c75-ad99-bde1625dd33e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282368} {:answer/id #uuid "7b04e125-fc05-5573-821b-ca2a9feac4a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "854b2cab-3ca7-5c75-ad99-bde1625dd33e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282368} :answer-field/id #uuid "59c8111f-f947-593b-8ef5-8a956ff68cf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "836fd02a-ccf5-58c6-ae6d-6f46ec341b1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88011" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)-16<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)-16 & <0 \\\\ 4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2) & <16 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(x+2) & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $4.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)} & <{4}^{4} \\\\ x+2 & <256 \\\\ x & <254\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+2>0\\,⇒\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<254,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,254).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,254)\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(-2,254)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-2,254),$ which can also be written as $-2<x<254.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6755ae6e-2434-5084-b91c-5f1e947222a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282372} {:answer/id #uuid "f44bfafe-31c1-5f05-a938-34aedee9dc0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282373} {:answer/id #uuid "19444b83-60e7-56d0-94cf-370654b4451c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282374} {:answer/id #uuid "6de068fc-ceb9-5de5-ab2b-5f682b763d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282375} {:answer/id #uuid "704ec1db-6bf5-504a-9abb-b0ef79a105a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f44bfafe-31c1-5f05-a938-34aedee9dc0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282373} :answer-field/id #uuid "71066fb2-8e7d-56c5-b1f3-6f84d8e6a588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9eb0c9bb-0c74-5a95-84fb-55928b1ffc95" :question/math-academy-id "q-88019" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{4}⁡(x-2)+1\\le {log}_{4}⁡(x+1).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $4.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x-2)+1} & \\le {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x+1)} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x-2)}\\cdot {4}^{1} & \\le {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x+1)} \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot 4 & \\le x+1 \\\\ 4x-8 & \\le x+1 \\\\ 3x & \\le 9 \\\\ x & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >2 \\\\ x+1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 3,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,3].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>2$ and $x>-1,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty )$ and $(-1,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3]\\cap (2,\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(2,3]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (2,3],$ which can also be written as $2<x\\le 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7086ac4-0841-5e9b-975f-f668fbd2c1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282379} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f15fe1-de52-5f54-8208-10871151ed61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282380} {:answer/id #uuid "0e443c42-f7c3-5a7d-9372-336d4a18c4b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282381} {:answer/id #uuid "db47709a-b0ea-5668-9018-7e6bbb5e1510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282382} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf07bbc-2a17-5959-a4cd-7e83c405f04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7086ac4-0841-5e9b-975f-f668fbd2c1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282379} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d06d4a6-6f0b-5ad4-ab3c-abf56357d3fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "655bfbdb-7719-5527-93d9-07954316b158" :question/math-academy-id "q-88022" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{5}⁡(6x)+2\\ge {log}_{5}⁡(3x)+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $5.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(6x)+2} & \\ge {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(3x)+3} \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(6x)}\\cdot {5}^{2} & \\ge {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\ 6x\\cdot 25 & \\ge 3x\\cdot 125 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 5x \\\\ -3x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0 \\\\ 3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 0,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,0].$ Additionally, the domain of both logarithms is $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(0,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0]\\cap (0,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319337f9-7abd-5450-82fd-5ba98710ba30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<x<\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282386} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ef25e4-af35-5b94-bde3-e24f8a8bb41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282387} {:answer/id #uuid "39499c94-8f4a-5365-ab5b-75b64ed3be7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282388} {:answer/id #uuid "52d9c87d-2970-518b-9a45-960ee4c4f17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282389} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e3086b-87ed-5b7a-98d7-00c90c0eded3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<x<\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1ef25e4-af35-5b94-bde3-e24f8a8bb41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282387} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a445833-b804-548d-80d1-18b5934452d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0f1922a-ec31-5441-90dc-f80b17594035" :question/math-academy-id "q-88040" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-4<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-4} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}\\cdot {e}^{-4} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)} \\\\ (x+1){e}^{-4} & <x-1 \\\\ (x+1) & <(x-1){e}^{4} \\\\ x+1 & <x{e}^{4}-{e}^{4} \\\\ 1+{e}^{4} & <x({e}^{4}-1) \\\\ \\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1} & <x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-1 \\\\ x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-1$ and $x>1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,\\infty )$ and $(1,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=(\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1}.$"}]}
